<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trustees for the Establishment of the Colony of Georgia in America</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1732">1732</a>–<a href="/years/1752">1752</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trustees for the Establishment of the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in America were the board, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-oglethorpe">James Oglethorpe</a>, that received a royal charter from George II in 1732 to found and govern Georgia. They aimed to settle the deserving poor and to shield <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> from Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, and they banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, hard liquor and large estates. Facing settler opposition, they lifted the bans and surrendered their charter to the Crown in 1752.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trustees-for-the-establishment-of-the-colony-of-georgia-in-america.jpg" alt="Trustees for the Establishment of the Colony of Georgia in America" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Verelst · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Audience_Given_by_the_Trustees_of_Georgia_to_a_Delegation_of_Creek_Indians.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In February 1729 Parliament set up a committee to investigate prison conditions, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-oglethorpe">James Edward Oglethorpe</a> as its chair. Its work led to the release of prisoners onto the streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and other cities with no prospect of work, and Oglethorpe conceived a colony as a way to employ such people usefully; the plan soon widened to other philanthropic and strategic aims. Dr. Thomas Bray, a supporter of prison reform, offered his organisation, the Associates of Dr. Bray, as the body to seek a charter, and Oglethorpe added members of the prison committee and other reformers.</p><p>After Bray died in February 1730 Oglethorpe became the driving force. Money from the D'Allone Legacy helped finance the trust. The petition for a royal charter was submitted in July 1731, George II signed the charter in April 1732, and it reached the trustees in June 1732.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Oglethorpe led the first colonists himself, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in November 1732 and arriving at the site of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> in February 1733. Although he and others had wanted debtors to people the colony, the Crown decided otherwise: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> was to be a military buffer for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> against the Spanish and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> factions. The settlers were chosen for useful skills, the men trained and enrolled in the militia, and most of the 114 travelled with wives, children and servants. The trustees laid down the Oglethorpe Plan, which set out how towns and districts were to be laid out, how land was to be shared and how the settlers were to defend themselves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The trustees named in the charter included Thomas Coram, John, Viscount Percival, later Earl of Egmont, and several members of parliament; more were added at annual meetings, beginning in March 1733. In London Benjamin Martyn served as secretary and Harman Verelst as accountant, while in Savannah William Stephens was secretary and later president of the colony. The trustees governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from its founding until 28 June 1752, the period known as Trustee Georgia. In 2008 the Georgia Historical Society revived the name, admitting two new Georgia Trustees each year for their service to the state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trustees for the Establishment of the Colony of Georgia in America” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1732 George II granted a charter to a board of <b>Trustees</b> led by James Oglethorpe for the land between the Savannah and Altamaha rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-georgia">Province of Georgia</a></li></ul></section>
